#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125381} :activity/id #uuid "62796b32-9fb1-56cf-9089-9689399b7402" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048533 :topic/id #uuid "a715d0ff-c3bf-59f0-8f15-f8770c11ebac" :topic/math-academy-id 1892} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125381 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125779 :tutorial/content "A **hyperboloid of one sheet** centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is a quadric surface whose equation is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =1,$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of one sheet\n\n$$x^2+y^2-z^2=1$$\n\nis shown below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/0d/0d97e5ac26685b49bcdb3155866bd34ba83bd758f02035b500d3f594fc5716c8.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the coordinate axes are as follows:\n\n- the $x$-intercepts are $(\\pm1,0,0)$\n- the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm1,0)$\n- there are no $z$-intercepts" :tutorial/id #uuid "b15b84bd-ab8f-5ade-8289-c1795374ab94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3438 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b1de06e-be8c-5534-9c53-59e61058ffb5" :step/math-academy-id 19457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125382 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124318 :question/id #uuid "e85cfe0b-4ff6-5cce-9384-aeb930843b49" :question/math-academy-id "e-5265"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b079a5ff-e73a-592f-b16c-8740682538f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050998}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378926}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid"} :step/id #uuid "4ba1a392-70de-526a-ac89-008dd6fbf4e5" :step/math-academy-id 19458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125465}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125781 :tutorial/content "A **hyperboloid of two sheets** centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =-1.$$\n\n**Watch out!** This equation is similar to the general equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet. The only difference is the right-hand side is now $-1$ instead of $+1.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of two sheets\n\n$$x^2+y^2-z^2=-1$$\n\nis represented below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,\\pm1).$ The surface does not intersect the $x$- or $y$-axes.\n\nCalculating the intercepts of a hyperboloid with the coordinate axes is done in the same way as we've seen before with spheres and ellipsoids. Let's see some examples.\n\nThere is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids of one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet: $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$$\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets: $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d9a4fb57-50db-54d3-a354-5c56c44b5b3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3440 :tutorial/title "Hyperboloids of Two Sheets"} :step/id #uuid "4ed11bf9-ec94-5810-9687-5899a45bf9cc" :step/math-academy-id 19462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124561 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124370 :question/id #uuid "1fbe37ed-9022-5c44-851c-0e66fe2fb79f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6078"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049705}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391957}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"} :step/id #uuid "fb6a05b0-ef9d-5743-a9b2-a3a300f5e53f" :step/math-academy-id 22380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125810 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered hyperboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when a hyperboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =1\n$$\n\n![](images/85/854a4e7506aa8e14fd19ceaadb5311a2d83596d27b33224272c41fa0639ea996.png)\n\n- When the $x$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =1\n$$\n\n![](images/ef/effc9d579d8d2ba00fec421fb1800639ccb4fba440e4518a5454d4ca282bc2d3.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the $y$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis.\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =-1\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b77432e38466d0983c196aed51eecd614096d971f21b0c68804e71a39548210.png)\n\n- When the $x$ term is negative, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} =-1\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d3b7e21da4acb5c13320c248c9cbe220488f43cfcbe328ad0416b22f449d143.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "37ea72a1-3f48-5ba4-94e5-ad5a2e962723" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3850 :tutorial/title "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "2deb75ff-8eff-541a-bff6-4a30ea363312" :step/math-academy-id 22397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124219 :question/id #uuid "58966f53-5f83-5c86-8ffe-38ad0dffd2b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401459}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"} :step/id #uuid "55a21c7e-3632-5bac-88a2-92a6bd3c409d" :step/math-academy-id 45274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125883 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperboloid of one sheet are either ellipses or hyperbolas.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of one sheet given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle centered at the origin of radius $1$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/b3/b369958a609ba3861be5d32fbf3195b6feb241228f81e89bb116b29f80e38318.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/4c/4c77b8758877ac88a73dece8c52f3b9cb650c4176edad5db020b138d70e0393b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/0d/0ddcfc2e78166fa801a46deccd30c4cc2ddc12e81ae2e81508b5e13a374c208f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3dfcfe4-330c-5142-8314-d09baa6e8acf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7836 :tutorial/title "Traces of Hyperboloids of One Sheet"} :step/id #uuid "f7656072-e089-540b-89c4-8df18945b7f8" :step/math-academy-id 45276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125884 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperboloid of two sheets are either ellipses, hyperbolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of two sheets given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=-1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is the empty set since the surface does not intersect the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/71/71d280f0b887b4805c882362dab8c9b13ef08b83f91904bce487ef749a67a6ab.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/34/3425d7f8df3a4dbbb5fa5763baf499e220215d0e33a0914859b8d1707074145d.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=1$ is a single point (the vertex of the upper sheet):\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 - 1^2 = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y,z) = (0,0,1)\n$$\n\nLikewise, the trace of our surface with the plane $z=-1$ is another point (the vertex of the lower sheet):\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 - (-1)^2 = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y,z) = (0,0,-1)\n$$\n\n- The trace of our surface with the horizontal plane $z=k$ for $|k| > 1$ is a circle:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 - k^2 = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow x^2+y^2 = k^2-1\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c91656e9656d4958de2b82f24ae05a6611d24d1cf0536384fbdd873d2e10e1d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "16cf67e7-24e7-5523-991e-af37acd35e60" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7837 :tutorial/title "Traces of Hyperboloids of Two Sheets"} :step/id #uuid "3e256a50-c4bf-5ba0-8f3e-b65f84c0fe2c" :step/math-academy-id 45277 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125885 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graphs a typical one-sheet and two-sheet hyperboloids centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/45/45359d1c73d9cdc721068dbee0e27bedb8cde622bae0615c16862e4157b2baf3.png)\n\nNotice that a hyperboloid of two sheets is defined for any $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ In fact, for any point $(x,y)$ in the $xy$-plane, there are exactly two points on the hyperboloid. \n\nOn the other hand, the graph of a hyperboloid of one sheet does not contain points over the central elliptical region (close to the axis of symmetry).\n\nFor example, given the hyperboloid of one sheet $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,$ let's find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$\n\nFrom the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the *exterior* and boundary of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ for the hyperboloid $x^2+y^2-z^2 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\left \\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\geq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d2a84b4-9a16-51d5-a343-bc7826406b8e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7838 :tutorial/title "The Domain of a Hyperboloid"} :step/id #uuid "203c24cd-0b43-5e99-91fc-7f9c44b7b75c" :step/math-academy-id 45281 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125382}}] :activity/title "Hyperboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124701}]}
